# Chapter 4 - Intune's Capabilities in Analytics and Automation
# From the book "AI, Automation and Analytics - Transforming Device Management
# with Intune" (2nd Edition) by Jannik Reinhard.
# https://github.com/JayRHa/intune-aaa-book-code
#
# The report export API as raw REST - the same thing export-report.ps1 automates.
# Pattern: start a job (POST), poll it (GET) until status == completed, then
# download the short-lived url. Run in Graph Explorer, or add an Authorization:
# Bearer <token> header.
#
# The endpoint is on v1.0 now, so you no longer reach for beta just to export.

@graph = https://graph.microsoft.com

### Step 1 - start the job. All discovered apps:
POST {{graph}}/v1.0/deviceManagement/reports/exportJobs
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reportName": "DevicesByAppInv",
  "localizationType": "LocalizedValuesAsAdditionalColumn"
}

### Step 1 (variant) - corporate-owned devices, only the device name column:
POST {{graph}}/v1.0/deviceManagement/reports/exportJobs
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reportName": "Devices",
  "filter": "(OwnerType eq '1')",
  "select": ["DeviceName"],
  "localizationType": "LocalizedValuesAsAdditionalColumn"
}

### Step 2 - poll by id until status reads "completed".
### The POST response returns a job id like Devices_a1b2c3d4-... - substitute it.
GET {{graph}}/v1.0/deviceManagement/reports/exportJobs('{jobId}')

### Step 3 - the completed response includes a short-lived "url" pointing at a
### zipped CSV (or JSON). Fetch it, unzip it, and you have your data.
